course: dictionary
lesson: 11667
title: Assertion: a strong claim
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An assertion is a statement that something is true. When someone makes an assertion, they say it firmly, as if it is a fact. «Tom said, "Dogs can fly."» That is an assertion. Tom is claiming it is true, but he has not shown any proof.
explain: Good readers and speakers ask: where is the proof? In English and debate, people back up their assertions with evidence, such as facts, examples or results. «Maya said, "Our river is getting dirtier." She showed the class photos and test results to back up her assertion.» Without evidence, an assertion is only a claim.
explain: The word also means standing up firmly for your rights or your opinions. «Priya made a clear assertion of her right to speak at the meeting.» She did not just hope to be heard. She stood up and said it.
explain: In science, an assertion should be tested before we believe it. In maths and computing, an assertion is a statement that must be true, and a program checks it. If the check fails, the program tells you something is wrong.
fact: An assertion is a strong statement that something is true, often without proof given.
fact: Assertion can also mean the act of standing up for your rights or opinions.
fact: In debate and English, good speakers back up their assertions with evidence.
fact: In science, an assertion should be tested, not just believed.
fact: In computing, an assertion is a statement that must be true, and the program checks it.
exercise: «Tom said, "Dogs can fly," and gave no proof.» What is Tom making? | A kind of song | A question about dogs | A list of facts, each with proof | *A strong statement that something is true, with no proof
exercise: In a debate, what should you do with an assertion? | *Back it up with evidence | Turn it into a question | Ignore it and sing a song | Write it in capital letters only
exercise: In a computer program, what is an assertion? | A bug that makes the screen go blank | A picture of a computer screen | A word that starts a loop | *A statement that must be true, which the program checks
test: Maya said, «My school is the best in town,» and gave no reason. What is her statement? | A poem about school | A question | *A claim that something is true, given without proof | A rude word
test: Which meaning fits «Ali made a firm assertion of his opinion»? | Drawing a map of the park | *Standing up firmly for his opinion | Running fast in a race | Sleeping late on a Sunday
exam: «Priya made a clear assertion of her right to be heard.» What does assertion mean here? | A question asking for help | Sleeping in on a school day | A picture of a river | *Standing up firmly for your rights or opinions
